#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180497} :activity/id #uuid "ff0789e0-f094-5fc5-ac25-230d7c590b14" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049147 :topic/id #uuid "de74c316-f607-5e04-8847-2af8b65a611b" :topic/math-academy-id 3694} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179739 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179601 :question/id #uuid "d5a9cb43-6396-5153-a60c-3d653d61a47b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9536"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dab6bfb9-608d-5ff8-88e7-5aa9612acda4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049853}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414785}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Constant Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "0c7bbec7-4f05-52c6-86b2-ee55d5eec5cf" :step/math-academy-id 35727 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180503}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180497 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180720 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ and $C$ is a smooth curve in the $xy$-plane. Recall that the line integral\n\n$$\nW = \\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$\n\nrepresents the total work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C.$ Loosely speaking:\n\n- if $W > 0,$ then $\\mathbf F$ causes the particle to *speed up* as it moves along $C,$ and\n\n- if $W < 0,$ then $\\mathbf F$ causes the particle to *slow down* as it moves along $C.$\n\nTo understand this, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the path $C$ defined by the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ shown below. \n\n![](images/2a/2a3b3896767e5f6beb4eec39f44afcc2387e178df0cec0ebbbdaa5e4cb68e964.png)\n\n \n\nIt's helpful to imagine that $\\mathbf F$ represents a uniform gravitational field near the earth's surface. \n\nLet's consider two examples.\n\n- If a particle moves from $A$ to $B$ along the segment, it will pick up speed because it moves *with* the vector field:\n\n![](images/0f/0f0bbc24f7ed26390643546bf578b88a879da58f56ac0b817159deeea25f164b.png)\n\n \nIntuitively, this makes sense. We know from real-world experience that if we drop a pebble, its speed increases as it descends. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW = \\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.\n$$\n\n- If a particle moves from $B$ to $A$ along the segment, it will slow down because it moves *against* the vector field:\n\n![](images/2e/2eabe985ee8b360ef79841561547b2251a818e63e53e91785a83d0c2cf3609e3.png)\n\nAgain, this matches our intuition: If we toss a pebble into the air, its speed decreases as it ascends.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW = \\int\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r < 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2e08c55c-9b68-57ce-97fe-81922244739a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5834 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2c33a62f-70ce-5226-b2d4-39e1df8540da" :step/math-academy-id 35733 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180498 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179742 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179604 :question/id #uuid "d844a573-49a1-59c3-bec4-e409c486469d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9542"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d72127a-90d6-5f7e-bd87-75d03285a918" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050885}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414786}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Non-Constant Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "e76a04e3-a2f3-5a0a-9547-3a509b4d4d5c" :step/math-academy-id 35747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180723 :tutorial/content "In some situations, a particle's speed increases and then decreases (or vice-versa) as it moves along a curve in a vector field. \n\nLet's consider a particle moving from $A$ to $B$ along the arc of a semicircle centered at $O$ in a uniform gravitational field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/46/466bd1716e626f6e3cc7859589974c27d49bdaa77bed9aa5813ef4bd9f3328e9.png)\n\n \n\nNote that:\n\n- as the particle moves from $A$ to $T,$ it works against $\\mathbf F$ and slows down, and\n\n- as the particle moves from $T$ to $B,$ it works with $\\mathbf F$ and speeds up.\n\nBy symmetry, the contribution to $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ for $x <                   0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x > 0.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =     0.\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this means that the particle's speed is the same at the points $A$ and $B,$ and these are the points of maximum speed. \n\nAdditionally, since $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ is decreasing as we move from $A$ to $T,$ the particle moves at its slowest speed at the point $T.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f659a6f3-f2aa-5170-a3d7-e5ba24fd59c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5843 :tutorial/title "Cases When a Particle Experiences Acceleration and Deceleration"} :step/id #uuid "dda3ff66-7674-5b97-99fb-85a94a2b066a" :step/math-academy-id 35802 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180493}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180724 :tutorial/content "Another noteworthy situation is when ![](images/c7/c75d4b37611dd632920692d430870b4d582fed06ac19178ccf69eb7c7dcb264a.svg) is perpendicular to ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) at all points along ![](images/27/278f99df1e14fe0ebfb61bdb72262a62670dcaa60d322148bc3ee591b278ec2d.svg)\n\n![](images/f3/f3f8b96e06c9e802e038130db69356924df9c9a385dc913e55cb5d9cc3a697b1.png)\n\n \n\nSince the force ![](images/c7/c75d4b37611dd632920692d430870b4d582fed06ac19178ccf69eb7c7dcb264a.svg) acts perpendicular to the particle's motion as it moves from ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) it neither helps nor hinders its motion. Therefore,\n\n![](images/06/06b0f05fc417c4572bb7763607ef1d471efc1282067b22c372f23caba33fcb19.svg)\n\nIn particular, if ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) is a level curve of a scalar field ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg) and ![](images/c5/c5b03af80a954012283edf6f027c02c7fe387d08ee6424e6b92186d580a65e7e.svg) then\n![](images/b3/b3dddb4aa6f178a9ab5ec8245e4881b260d60cfba33123b56b8b5ea99cda95d8.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "13e79581-4a65-5db7-9362-f065ba606386" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5844 :tutorial/title "Cases When the Vector Field and Curve are Perpendicular"} :step/id #uuid "51bd906d-35f0-5627-ad2d-ac5987f58545" :step/math-academy-id 35803 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180498}}] :activity/title "Interpreting Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179848}]}
